I. What a Bandit Brings
Chōbei didn’t knock.

He never did.

By the third night, he stopped announcing himself altogether.
Just appeared—like something the forest had decided to spit out.

This time—
he brought something.

He tossed it.

It landed beside Tsukihiko’s paints with a dull, solid sound.

A small wooden box.
Carved.
Worn.
Expensive.

Stolen.

Tsukihiko didn’t look at it immediately.
Brush first.
Line finished.
Water set.

Then—
his gaze flicked down.

“…You stole that,” he said.

Chōbei dropped onto the stone beside him, stretching out like he belonged there.
“Yeah.”

No shame.
No apology.

“…Why.”

Chōbei shrugged, eyes half-lidded, watching him from the side.
“Thought it’d look better here than wherever it was.”

A pause.

“…You don’t even know what it is,” Tsukihiko said.

Chōbei grinned.
“Don’t need to.”

Silence.

Tsukihiko opened it.

Inside—
pigments.
Rare ones.
Deep mineral blues.
Crushed silver.
A red so rich it looked like it had been pulled from something living.

His brush stilled.

Just for a moment.

Chōbei noticed.
Of course he did.

“…You use cheap stuff,” he said casually.

Tsukihiko closed the box.

“…You’re trying to buy something.”

Chōbei snorted.
“Relax.”
A beat.

“I don’t buy things.”

His gaze slid back to him—sharp now.
Intent.

“I take them.”

Another pause.

“…Then what is this?” Tsukihiko asked.

Chōbei leaned back on his hands.
Looked at the sky.

“…Investment,” he said.

Tsukihiko studied him.
Longer this time.

Then—
quietly—

“…You’re inefficient.”

Chōbei laughed.

“Yeah?” he shot back. “And you’re still using it.”

Tsukihiko didn’t answer.

But he didn’t push the box away either.

II. The Moon Tests What Stays
The next time—
Tsukihiko wasn’t on the terrace.

Chōbei noticed immediately.

He stood there for a second.
Clicking his tongue.

“…Tch. Figures.”

He didn’t leave.

Instead—
he wandered.

Through the estate.

No guards stopped him.
No one screamed.

That was wrong.

That meant one thing.

He was being allowed.

“…You’re annoying,” Tsukihiko’s voice came from somewhere above him.

Chōbei looked up.

There—
in the branches of a wide tree.

Reclined like he’d always been there.

“…You’re hiding now?” Chōbei called.

“No.”
Pause.

“…You’re looking.”

That—
made him grin.

“Yeah,” Chōbei said. “I am.”

He climbed.

Not elegantly.
Not carefully.

Just fast.

He landed on the branch across from him, balanced easily, crouched like something feral.

“…So what, I pass?” he asked.

Tsukihiko watched him.
Still.
Measured.

“You came back without being invited.”

“Yeah.”

“You stayed when I wasn’t there.”

“Yeah.”

“You didn’t take anything.”

A beat.

Chōbei smirked.
“…Yet.”

That—
earned him something.

Not a smile.
Not quite.

But close.

“…You’re consistent,” Tsukihiko said.

“Yeah.”

“…That’s rare.”

Chōbei tilted his head.
Studying him now.

“…You testing me?” he asked.

Tsukihiko didn’t answer.

That was answer enough.

III. Teeth That Don’t Bite—Yet
It happened without warning.

A servant.

Young.
Nervous.

She approached the terrace one evening, carrying fresh water for the paints.

She didn’t see Chōbei at first.

But when she did—
she froze.

“…M-my lord—” she started, eyes darting to Tsukihiko.

Chōbei’s grin came slow.
Lazy.
Sharp.

“…Relax,” he drawled.

She didn’t relax.

Her fear—
sharp.
Obvious.

It shifted the air.

Tsukihiko noticed.

Of course he did.

“…Leave it,” he said gently to her.

She set the tray down quickly and left.
Too quickly.

Silence.

Chōbei watched her go.

Then—
“…You don’t like that,” he said.

Tsukihiko didn’t answer immediately.

“…She was afraid,” he said finally.

Chōbei snorted.
“Yeah. That happens.”

A pause.

Then—
Tsukihiko looked at him.
Directly.

“You enjoy that.”

Not a question.

Chōbei didn’t deny it.

“Depends,” he said.

“…On what.”

Chōbei’s eyes flicked to him.
Sharp again.

“…If it’s worth it.”

Silence.

The kind that stretches.
Tests.

Then—
Tsukihiko spoke.

“…If you make this place unsafe,” he said quietly, “you won’t come back.”

Flat.
Certain.

Chōbei stared at him.

Then—
slowly—

he grinned.

“…So that’s the rule?”

“…It’s not a rule.”

A pause.

“…It’s a boundary.”

That—
that landed.

Chōbei leaned back again.

“…Alright,” he said.

Simple.
Easy.

But real.

IV. Something Like Territory
It didn’t take long.

It never does.

Another man.

Not a threat.
Not really.

A minor noble’s son.
Pretty.
Polished.

He came during the day.

Too clean.
Too careful.

And he looked at Tsukihiko—
like he wanted something.

Chōbei saw it immediately.

He was in the trees.
Watching.

The way the man stood too close.
Spoke too soft.
Smiled too much.

Tsukihiko tolerated it.
Calm.
Polite.

But not interested.

That didn’t matter.

Chōbei dropped down.

Right between them.

Uninvited.

“…You’re in the way,” the noble snapped.

Chōbei didn’t even look at him.

“…Yeah,” he said.

Then—
he stepped closer to Tsukihiko.

Too close.
Again.

“Thought you didn’t like loud people,” he added, voice casual, but his presence—
sharp.
Claiming.

Tsukihiko’s eyes flicked to him.

Then to the noble.

“…You should leave,” Tsukihiko said calmly.

The noble stiffened.
“…I was invited—”

“You’re not anymore.”

Flat.
Done.

The man left.

Quickly.

Silence returned.

Chōbei exhaled through his nose.

“…Annoying,” he muttered.

Tsukihiko studied him.

“…You interfered.”

“Yeah.”

“…Why.”

Chōbei looked at him like the answer was obvious.

“…Didn’t like him.”

A pause.

“…That’s not a reason.”

Chōbei grinned.

“Felt like one.”

Silence stretched again.

Then—
Tsukihiko spoke.

“…You’re territorial.”

Chōbei didn’t deny it.

“…You let me be,” he said instead.

That was the real question.

Tsukihiko’s gaze lingered on him.
Longer this time.

Then—
quietly—

“…For now.”

Chōbei’s grin came back.

Slow.
Satisfied.

“Good.”

And this time—
when the night settled in—

he stayed without asking.

And Tsukihiko didn’t send him away.

